{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Knot.AngleStructureUniqueness","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Knot.AngleStructureUniqueness","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.Convex.Jensen","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic","lean:module:QOU.Knot.SymmetricOptimum","lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.IdealTetrahedronVolumeConcave"],"declarations":4,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.AngleStructureUniqueness.tetVol_maximizer_unique","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.AngleStructureUniqueness.tetVol_maximizer_unique","kind":"theorem","line":160,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/AngleStructureUniqueness.lean#L160-L169","doc":"**Rivin Thm 1.4 for the single ideal tetrahedron.** The Lobachevsky volume\n`tetVol` of an ideal tetrahedron has **at most one** maximiser on the open angle\nsimplex `tetSimplex`.  Discharged sorry-free from the strict concavity\n`tetVol_strictConcaveOn` (bean `qou-idvt` T3-plumbing) via the generic\nuniqueness mechanism above — the analytic half of `qou-krvm` (ii) for the\none-tetrahedron anchor. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.AngleStructureUniqueness.strictConcave_symmetric_maximizer_unique","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.AngleStructureUniqueness.strictConcave_symmetric_maximizer_unique","kind":"theorem","line":139,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/AngleStructureUniqueness.lean#L139-L153","doc":"**Unique symmetric maximiser (the `qou-krvm` (ii) statement, corrected).**\n\nA strictly concave, symmetry-invariant objective on a convex, symmetry-stable\nfeasible set has a maximiser that is both **unique** and **symmetric**.  This is\nwhat pins the LP optimum and the geometric (volume-maximising) point to a single\ncommon point — the vanishing-offset explanation — with uniqueness coming from\nstrict concavity rather than from the symmetry fixing a lone point. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.AngleStructureUniqueness.strictConcave_maximizer_symmetric","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.AngleStructureUniqueness.strictConcave_maximizer_symmetric","kind":"theorem","line":116,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/AngleStructureUniqueness.lean#L116-L137","doc":"**A maximiser of a strictly-concave invariant objective is a symmetric\npoint.**\n\nGiven a maximiser `p`, its orbit average is feasible (`orbitAvg_mem`),\nsymmetry-fixed (`orbitAvg_fixed`), and — since `f` is concave and invariant —\ndoes not decrease `f` (`le_apply_orbitAvg`).  So the orbit average is *also* a\nmaximiser; strict-concavity uniqueness (`strictConcaveOn_maximizer_unique`)\nforces it to equal `p`, whence `p` is fixed by every group element. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.AngleStructureUniqueness.strictConcaveOn_maximizer_unique","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.AngleStructureUniqueness.strictConcaveOn_maximizer_unique","kind":"theorem","line":85,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/AngleStructureUniqueness.lean#L85-L105","doc":"**Uniqueness of the maximiser of a strictly concave objective.**\n\nIf `f` is strictly concave on the convex set `K` and both `p` and `q` maximise\n`f` over `K`, then `p = q`.  The midpoint of two distinct maximisers would beat\nthem by strict concavity, contradicting maximality.  This is the analytic core\nof Rivin's uniqueness theorem: strict concavity, by itself, forbids a second\noptimum. ","signatureChunk":0}]}